From 9ff6d4c6d6f84d6f1c08665aaaa6c38a59253dc7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 21:23:42 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 5/8] SignalDelegator: keep ignored signals ignored across
 execve

execve resets caught signals to SIG_DFL but leaves SIG_IGN in place, which
is how `trap '' HUP; exec prog`, nohup and a parent's ignored SIGPIPE reach
a child. A guest execve starts a new FEX, whose SignalDelegator began every
GuestAction value-initialised (SIG_DFL); the inherited disposition only
landed in OldAction when a host thunk was installed. So a program started
with a signal ignored saw SIG_DFL from sigaction(), and lost the ignore as
soon as it saved and restored the old action. The host disposition itself
stayed SIG_IGN, so delivery was right until then.

The constructor now reads the inherited host disposition of every signal
it has no required handler for and records SIG_IGN in the guest action.

Evidence, 64K host, FEX_HOSTPAGEMODE=force, FEX_PORTABLE=1 so the child
runs under the same build: a static x86-64 test ignores SIGUSR2, execs
itself, and the child queries SIGUSR2:
  before: usr2-ignore-kept=no
  after:  usr2-ignore-kept=yes
Linux (the same test on an aarch64 Raspberry Pi 5): yes. With the fix,
Ubuntu 24.04 bash started with `trap '' USR2` reports `trap -- '' SIGUSR2`
and survives `kill -USR2 $$`.

Found in POWERarm by the A64Syscalls sys_fork_exec test.
---
 .../LinuxSyscalls/SignalDelegator.cpp             | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SignalDelegator.cpp b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SignalDelegator.cpp
index 3cb0d2bff..7ac5c3edf 100644
--- a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SignalDelegator.cpp
+++ b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SignalDelegator.cpp
@@ -2155,6 +2155,21 @@ SignalDelegator::SignalDelegator(FEXCore::Context::Context* _CTX, const std::str
   for (uint32_t Signal = 0; Signal <= SignalDelegator::MAX_SIGNALS; ++Signal) {
     RegisterHostSignalHandlerForGuest(Signal, GuestSignalHandler);
   }
+
+  // execve keeps ignored signals ignored (the kernel only resets caught ones
+  // to SIG_DFL), and this process is the guest's execve: seed the guest view
+  // from the dispositions it inherited. Nothing has installed a host handler
+  // for the non-required signals yet, so the host still holds exactly what the
+  // exec'ing process left.
+  for (uint32_t Signal = 1; Signal <= SignalDelegator::MAX_SIGNALS; ++Signal) {
+    if (Signal == SIGKILL || Signal == SIGSTOP || HostHandlers[Signal].Required.load(std::memory_order_relaxed)) {
+      continue;
+    }
+    GuestSigAction Inherited {};
+    if (::syscall(SYS_rt_sigaction, Signal, nullptr, &Inherited, 8) == 0 && Inherited.sigaction_handler.handler == SIG_IGN) {
+      HostHandlers[Signal].GuestAction.sigaction_handler.handler = SIG_IGN;
+    }
+  }
 }
 
 SignalDelegator::~SignalDelegator() {
